# CQ 24: Which of the named elements carry no identification?
#
# The counterpart of question 23. Part 1, 4.4 provides the identification for
# the elements a description names, and isIdentifiedBy is functional, so an
# element carries at most one.
#
# Restricted to the four kinds a reader refers to by name: the process, its
# operators, the states passing between them, and the resources performing
# them. The flow is deliberately left out. It is the line between two named
# things and is read off its endpoints, so an unidentified flow is the normal
# case and reporting it would bury the cases that matter.
#
# What the standard does not do is require an identification. A model may leave
# one out, and OWL cannot object under the open world assumption: an absent
# identification means unstated, not absent. The answer therefore reports what
# a model has left open rather than what it has got wrong, and enforcing it is
# a matter for SHACL.
PREFIX vdi3682: <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#>
PREFIX rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
SELECT ?element ?elementKind
WHERE {
  VALUES ?root { vdi3682:ProcessOperator vdi3682:Process vdi3682:State
                 vdi3682:TechnicalResource }
  ?element a ?elementKind . ?elementKind rdfs:subClassOf* ?root .
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?element vdi3682:isIdentifiedBy ?any }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?element a ?narrower . ?narrower rdfs:subClassOf+ ?elementKind }
} ORDER BY ?elementKind ?element
